---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import Container from "@components/Container.astro";
import Layout from "@layouts/Layout.astro";
import ImagePopout from "@components/ImagePopout.astro";
import StaticGraphic from "@components/StaticGraphic.astro";
import MultiCol from "@components/MultiCol.astro";
import Gap from "@components/Gap.astro";
import Nav from "@components/Nav.astro"
import Footer from "@components/Footer.astro"

import Thumb from './thumbnailbg.png';
import ThumbText from './thumbnailtype.png';
import Gallery from '@assets/images/galleryText.png';
import GalBG1 from "@assets/images/gallery1.png"
import GalBG2 from "@assets/images/gallery2.png"
import DrewTwitch from './drew-twitch.png';
import DrewRender from './drew-render.png';
import TeamGraphic from './mrblue-final.png';
import TeamRender from './mrblue-render.png';
import ModelImport from './hulkimport.png';
import ModelPose from './hulkpose.png';
import ModelAdjust from './hulkadjust.png';
import ModelRender from './hulkrender.png';
import ModelShowcase from './hulkmodelshowcase.gif';
import SkipLink from "@components/SkipLink.astro";

---

<Layout title= "Event Design">
  <Nav hLink="../../" />
  <div id="gallery">
    <ImagePopout 
      image={Gallery} 
      alt="Opener for the image gallery" 
      w="210px" 
      galleryID="1"
    />
    <StaticGraphic 
      i1={GalBG1} 
      i2={GalBG2} 
      b="70px" 
      r="80px" 
      w="90px"
    />
  </div>

  <div class="content-contain">
    <ImagePopout image={Thumb} alt="Event Design Thumbnail" w="100%" galleryID="2"/>
    <div class="textgroup">
      <h1 class="header">3D Compositing</h1>
      <p>
        For this project I took on two 3D based assignments, the first one I’d like to talk about is a twitch banner I did for a Twitch streamer named Drewsephix. When talking about the use of 3D in Esports,
        oftentimes it comes down to the fact that in Esports players are frequently represented by the character that they play in their respective games.
        As such, a lot of graphics focusing on these players also implement the character they play in some way. The most organic-looking way to do this is through what is called 3D rigging and 3D compositing.
        3D rigging involves taking the model of a character and its “skeleton”, which is what animators use to pose a 3D model, and posing it in a way that translates well for the graphic. 3D compositing is using the engine’s
        lighting and texture tools to produce a final render that matches or mimics the lighting and coloring of the design it is being used for.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="textgroup">
      <h2>Twitch Graphic Compositing Timelapse</h2>
      <p>
        For this first project I am working with the model of “Orcane” from the game “Rivals of Aether 2” for the purposes of creating a profile banner image.
        Instead of directly explaining the steps to you here, check out the timelapse I have linked below to see my process!
      </p>
    </div>
    <iframe
      width="1080" 
      height="720" 
      src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/FENQ91GEr_g" 
      class="yt-video"
      title="Drewsephix Banner Timelapse - Esports Visualized" frameborder="0" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share" referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin" allowfullscreen>
    </iframe>
    <div class="textgroup">
      <p>
        The timelapse above shows the general process. Take the model, pose it how you’d like, make the colors and lighting match, and then render it out to use in your design!
        For this design we were sticking with a simpler color scheme aligning with the red and black on the character which allowed me to be a lot more loose with the lighting in the 3D software.
        You can see how the final banner turned out below and what the character render alone came out as!
      </p>
    </div>
    <MultiCol>
      <ImagePopout image={DrewRender} alt="New Paltz Esports' Icon Logo" w="600px" galleryID="1"/>
    </MultiCol>
    <MultiCol>
      <ImagePopout image={DrewTwitch} alt="New Paltz Esports' Icon Logo" w="800px" galleryID="1"/>
    </MultiCol>
    <div class="textgroup">
      <p>
        This project serves as a good example for what most player-centric graphics that involve 3D models will look like. Through use of the character that represents them it becomes more easily recognizable to outside viewers.
        Freeform posing allows you to create distinct compositions that match the energy of the type of design you’re making. 
        And through the lighting and compositing of the model you can allow it to seamlessly fit into the visual identity of any design you create.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="textgroup">
      <h2>Team Graphic 3D Compositing</h2>
      <p>
        My next project takes the same principles and turns things up to include a few more characters and lighting techniques to show how far 3D Compositing can truly go.
        This project is a team graphic for SUNY New Paltz’s Marvel Rivals team. In the game “Marvel Rivals” each team is composed of six players, in this case seven due to a substitute player,
        and each player can select from loads of different Marvel characters and skins for those characters. In this case I asked each team member for the preferred
        character and skin they would like on the graphic and compiled a seven-character graphic to showcase the entire team! You can see below the final graphic.
      </p>
    </div>
    <MultiCol>
      <ImagePopout image={TeamGraphic} alt="New Paltz Esports' Icon Logo" w="800px" galleryID="3"/>
    </MultiCol>
    <div class="textgroup">
      <p>
        With a seven-character graphic like this one rigging and posing all of the individual models would take ages, which is why there’s a much easier way to handle it depending on the game your working with.
        The easier way involves similar techniques to what we learned before but involves taking animations straight from the game itself as a baseline, thankfully Marvel Rivals is one of the games we can do this with so let me walk you through the process.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="textgroup">
      <p>
        The first step is to import the model into your 3D software of choice. This is similar to how we handled the character in the previous project. For software I use Blender and to get the models from the game I use RivalsPorting.
        You can see what the imported model looks like below:
      </p>
    </div>
    <MultiCol>
      <ImagePopout image={ModelImport} alt="New Paltz Esports' Icon Logo" w="500px" galleryID="1"/>
    </MultiCol>
    <div class="textgroup">
      <p>
        Now, instead of posing the entire body of the model, export the character’s animations from wherever you exported the character’s model.
        Then take those animations and apply them to the skeleton of the character model until you find a pose from one of those animations that you like.
        This saves you the entire posing process by skipping straight to a mostly finished pose, you can see the one I chose for this Hulk here:
      </p>
    </div>
    <MultiCol>
      <ImagePopout image={ModelPose} alt="New Paltz Esports' Icon Logo" w="500px" galleryID="1"/>
    </MultiCol>
    <div class="textgroup">
      <p>
        Next, check the animation, make sure you isolate the frame you want and delete the rest. This will allow you to now tweak the pose of the existing animation to your liking,
        as oftentimes you aren't guaranteed to find the perfect pose out of only the game’s animations. For this instance I move the arms down, made the face point towards the camera and adjusted his facial features.
      </p>
    </div>
    <MultiCol>
      <ImagePopout image={ModelAdjust} alt="New Paltz Esports' Icon Logo" w="500px" galleryID="1"/>
    </MultiCol>
    <div class="textgroup">
      <p>
        Now lastly we can go in and fix any texture issues that the model may have and set up anything with our lighting and our compositing! Lighting the model is very important to bringing out the pose as shown
        in the previous project so make sure to carefully consider the context of where the render will go and light accordingly. You can see the rendered image here.
      </p>
    </div>
    <MultiCol>
      <ImagePopout image={ModelRender} alt="New Paltz Esports' Icon Logo" w="700px" galleryID="1"/>
    </MultiCol>
    <div class="textgroup">
      <p>
        This method cuts what would normally take about an hour per character down to a third of that time. Starting from the baseline of an already competently made animation makes things much easier and
        due to the nature of 3D these poses and positions are all still completely editable so you’re not under any restrictions using this method either!
        Lastly here as a showcase of the power of this method, you can see a roundabout of the entire model fully lit.
      </p>
    </div>
    <MultiCol>
      <ImagePopout image={ModelShowcase} alt="New Paltz Esports' Icon Logo" w="800px" galleryID="1"/>
    </MultiCol>
    <div class="textgroup">
      <p>
        I used this method on all seven characters for the team graphic, lit them according to the graphic I wanted to place them in, and rendered them as you can see below!
      </p>
    </div>
    <MultiCol>
      <ImagePopout image={TeamRender} alt="New Paltz Esports' Icon Logo" w="800px" galleryID="1"/>
    </MultiCol>
    <div class="textgroup">
      <p>
        And this of course allowed me to create the final graphic as seen above.
      </p>
    </div>
    <MultiCol>
      <ImagePopout image={TeamGraphic} alt="New Paltz Esports' Icon Logo" w="800px" galleryID="1"/>
    </MultiCol>
    <div class="textgroup">
      <h2>Conclusion</h2>
      <p>
        3D Posing and Compositing is an extremely useful and sometimes required skill when designing for Esports. That being said it also leads the designs it features in to really stand out as their own style.
        The representation of players by their respective characters allows for much more freedom using these 3D skills. Instead of needing to work with specific photography of your players
        you can work with a fully posable and customizable asset which is an experience not found in many other industries. This is a skill I’d highly recommend to anyone interested in designing for Esports,
        it has allowed me to create some of my favorite works and has given me so much freedom to enact my creative vision!
      </p>
    </div>
  <Footer />
  </div>
</Layout>

<script>
</script>

<style>
  .page-contain{
    display: flex;
    align-items: top;
    /* grid-template-columns: 650px 1fr; */
    justify-content: center;
    margin: var(--size-page-margin) 0;
  }
  .content-contain{
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    margin: 0 auto;
    gap: 3rem;
    background-color: var(--color-background);
    height: 100%;
    padding: 3rem;
    padding-top: 8rem;
    max-width: 1200px;
    clip-path: polygon(50% 0, 50% 0, 50% 100%, 50% 100%);
    animation: Open 1s both;
  }
  .content-contain p { max-width: 800px; }

  @keyframes Open{
    0%    { clip-path: polygon(50% 0, 50% 0, 50% 100%, 50% 100%); }
    100%  { clip-path: polygon(0 0, 100% 0, 100% 100%, 0 100%); }
  }

  .textgroup h2 { color: var(--color-text); font-family: var(--font-headings);}
  .gallery-contain{
    width: 800px;
    padding: 1rem;
    background-color: var(--color-background);
  }

  #gallery{
    position: fixed;
    padding: 1rem;
    right: 40px;
    top: -40px;
    z-index: 1000;
  }
  
  .header{
    text-align: center;
    margin-bottom: 10px;
    font-size: 60pt;
    font-weight: 900;
  }

  .yt-video {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
  }

  @media (max-width: 800px) {
    #gallery{
      display: none;
    }
    p{
      font-size: 12pt;
    }
    .content-contain{
      padding: 1rem;
      padding-top: 8rem;
    }
    .header{
      font-size: clamp(24pt, 10vw, 48pt);
    }
  }
</style>